{"causes":["Parkinson's Law arose from C. Northcote Parkinson's observations on human behavior and work productivity in the mid-20th century.","The concept was initially presented in a 1955 article titled &quot;Parkinson's Law: On Intelligence, Survival &amp; Production in War,&quot; which critiqued the British establishment's performance during World War II.","The law was further popularized through Parkinson's subsequent writings and lectures on management and productivity."],"effects":["Parkinson's Law has influenced organizational management and productivity by highlighting the importance of time management and resource allocation.","The concept has been used to explain why some organizations struggle with efficiency and effectiveness, leading to calls for better time management practices and more effective resource utilization.","Parkinson's Law continues to inspire research into human behavior and work productivity, shedding light on the psychological factors that contribute to procrastination and decreased efficiency."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"c-northcote-parkinson","sourceName":"C. Northcote Parkinson","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"parkinsons-law","targetName":"Parkinson's Law","context":"Parkinson created the concept of Parkinson's Law in his 1955 article "Parkinson's Law: On Intelligence, Survival &amp; Production in War.""},{"sourceSlug":"world-war-ii","sourceName":"World War II","verb":"OCCURS_DURING","targetSlug":"parkinsons-law","targetName":"Parkinson's Law","context":"Parkinson's Law was initially presented in a 1955 article critiquing the British establishment's performance during World War II."},{"sourceSlug":"british-establishment","sourceName":"British Establishment","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"parkinsons-law","targetName":"Parkinson's Law","context":"Parkinson's Law was initially presented in a critique of the British establishment's performance during World War II."}],"places":[{"name":"United Kingdom","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Great Britain","role":"Place of Publication"},{"name":"London, United Kingdom","role":"Location of publication"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Parkinson's law","type":"Reference article","slug":"parkinsons-law"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q506207","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Parkinson%27s_law"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Parkinson's law contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Parkinson's Law is a satirical adage and a book (full title: Parkinson's Law, or The Pursuit of Progress) by the British historian, naval historian, and management satirist C. Northcote Parkinson (1909–1993), derived from an essay first published in The Economist on 19 November 1955 and expanded into a book published by Houghton Mifflin in 1958. Parkinson's Law states: 'Work expands so as to fill the time available for its completion.' The essay and book argue, through mock-scholarly historical analysis of the British Admiralty and Colonial Office, that the number of officials in any bureaucracy tends to increase at a fixed annual rate (5.17% to 6.56% per year, Parkinson claims with satirical statistical precision), regardless of the actual work to be done — bureaucratic expansion is self-perpetuating, driven by two motivating factors: the desire of officials to multiply their subordinates (rather than their rivals), and the tendency for officials to create work for each other.

Parkinson's Law is one of the most widely cited aphorisms in management theory and organisational science — its formulation of the tendency of bureaucratic expansion to outpace productive need has been verified by empirical research and applied to fields ranging from government administration through software development (in Scrum and Agile methodologies, where Parkinson's Law is cited as a reason for time-boxing tasks) to personal productivity (in time management, where the principle suggests that deadlines should be set aggressively to prevent work from expanding).

Parkinson's corollary laws — 'Expenditure rises to meet income', 'Delay is the deadliest form of denial', and 'Officials want to multiply subordinates, not rivals' — have similarly entered the lexicon of management theory. The book was a satirical bestseller in Britain and the United States and remains in print; Parkinson's Law has entered the English language as a widely understood idiom for bureaucratic self-perpetuation.","subjects":["British Literature","Modern Era","C. Northcote Parkinson","Management Theory","Bureaucracy","Satire","Organisational Science","20th Century"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Parkinson's Law has had a lasting impact on organizational management and productivity, influencing the way people think about time management and resource allocation.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
